 (const@G,k,vxbeasts.nim :strlit.0.I11783052994293755587. . LongString.0.sysvq0asl
  (oconstr LongString.0.sysvq0asl
   (kv fullLen.0 11068)
   (kv rc.0 0)
   (kv capImpl.0 0)
   (kv data.0 "\23 The grammar is `screen.conf`\27s\3A `\23 ...` is a comment and every other line is\0A\23 an id followed by `field=value` pairs. An id with a DOT in it is a box of the\0A\23 model named before the dot; an id without one is the model itself. See\0A\23 `vxmodel.nim` for what every field means.\0A\0A\23 --------------------------------------------------------------------------\0A\23 The player, and everybody shaped like one.\0A\23\0A\23 These six boxes are the ones `vxskin.nim` used to hold as literals. It reads\0A\23 them from here now, so there is one spelling\3A `Tests/view_test.exe` proves\0A\23 the unwrap of exactly these rows and did not change a line when they moved.\0A\0Aplayer                  tex=64,64 scale=0.9375 height=1.8 plate=1\0Aplayer.head             at=-4,24,-4,4,32,4   pivot=0,24,0  uv=0,0   role=head   rgb=\23c79978\0Aplayer.trunk            at=-4,12,-2,4,24,2   pivot=0,24,0  uv=16,16             rgb=\2300abb5\0Aplayer.rightArm         at=4,12,-2,8,24,2    pivot=5,22,0  uv=40,16 role=armR   turn=45 rgb=\23c79978\0Aplayer.leftArm          at=-8,12,-2,-4,24,2  pivot=-5,22,0 uv=32,48 role=armL   turn=45 rgb=\23c79978\0Aplayer.rightLeg         at=0,0,-2,4,12,2     pivot=2,12,0  uv=0,16  role=legR   turn=45 rgb=\233d4587\0Aplayer.leftLeg          at=-4,0,-2,0,12,2    pivot=-2,12,0 uv=16,48 role=legL   turn=45 rgb=\233d4587\0A\0A\23 A zombie is a player with its arms held out in front of it. Same six boxes,\0A\23 same rectangles, one field different - which is the whole argument for the\0A\23 models being data rather than code.\0Azombie                  tex=64,64 scale=0.9375 height=1.95 plate=1\0Azombie.head             at=-4,24,-4,4,32,4   pivot=0,24,0  uv=0,0   role=head   rgb=\2300afaf\0Azombie.trunk            at=-4,12,-2,4,24,2   pivot=0,24,0  uv=16,16             rgb=\2300a8a8\0Azombie.rightArm         at=4,12,-2,8,24,2    pivot=5,22,0  uv=40,16 role=armR   turn=18 hold=-90 rgb=\2300afaf\0Azombie.leftArm          at=-8,12,-2,-4,24,2  pivot=-5,22,0 uv=32,48 role=armL   turn=18 hold=-90 rgb=\2300afaf\0Azombie.rightLeg         at=0,0,-2,4,12,2     pivot=2,12,0  uv=0,16  role=legR   turn=45 rgb=\232d4a8a\0Azombie.leftLeg          at=-4,0,-2,0,12,2    pivot=-2,12,0 uv=16,48 role=legL   turn=45 rgb=\232d4a8a\0A\0A\23 A skeleton is the same layout with two-pixel limbs, on a 64x32 picture where\0A\23 the two arms read one rectangle and the two legs read another. That is what a\0A\23 pre-1.8 layout is, and it is not a special case here\3A the rectangle a box\0A\23 reads is a field, so two boxes reading one rectangle is two rows with the\0A\23 same `uv`.\0Askeleton                tex=64,32 scale=0.9375 height=1.99 plate=1\0Askeleton.head           at=-4,24,-4,4,32,4   pivot=0,24,0  uv=0,0   role=head   rgb=\23c1c1c1\0Askeleton.trunk          at=-4,12,-2,4,24,2   pivot=0,24,0  uv=16,16             rgb=\23b4b4b4\0Askeleton.rightArm       at=4,12,-1,6,24,1    pivot=5,23,0  uv=40,16 role=armR   turn=45 rgb=\23c1c1c1\0Askeleton.leftArm        at=-6,12,-1,-4,24,1  pivot=-5,23,0 uv=40,16 role=armL   turn=45 rgb=\23c1c1c1\0Askeleton.rightLeg       at=1,0,-1,3,12,1     pivot=2,12,0  uv=0,16  role=legR   turn=45 rgb=\23b4b4b4\0Askeleton.leftLeg        at=-3,0,-1,-1,12,1   pivot=-2,12,0 uv=0,16  role=legL   turn=45 rgb=\23b4b4b4\0A\0A\23 --------------------------------------------------------------------------\0A\23 A creeper\3A a body on four short legs, and no arms at all.\0A\23\0A\23 The four legs are two pairs and not four independents - front-right walks\0A\23 with back-left, which is how anything on four legs walks - so the diagonals\0A\23 share a role and the roles do the rest.\0A\0Acreeper                 tex=64,32 scale=1.0 height=1.7 plate=1\0Acreeper.head            at=-4,18,-4,4,26,4   pivot=0,18,0  uv=0,0   role=head   rgb=\234f9c3c\0Acreeper.trunk           at=-4,6,-2,4,18,2    pivot=0,18,0  uv=16,16             rgb=\234f9c3c\0Acreeper.frontRightLeg   at=0,0,-6,4,6,-2     pivot=2,6,-4  uv=0,16  role=legR   turn=45 rgb=\233f8330\0Acreeper.frontLeftLeg    at=-4,0,-6,0,6,-2    pivot=-2,6,-4 uv=0,16  role=legL   turn=45 rgb=\233f8330\0Acreeper.backRightLeg    at=0,0,2,4,6,6       pivot=2,6,4   uv=0,16  role=legL   turn=45 rgb=\233f8330\0Acreeper.backLeftLeg     at=-4,0,2,0,6,6      pivot=-2,6,4  uv=0,16  role=legR   turn=45 rgb=\233f8330\0A\0A\23 --------------------------------------------------------------------------\0A\23 A spider\3A a head, a thorax, an abdomen and eight legs in a ring.\0A\23\0A\23 `turn` on a `crawl` box is not an angle - it is which of the eight that leg\0A\23 is, and `vxanim.crawlYaw` turns a place in the ring into two angles. Eight\0A\23 legs each with their own sine is what Minecraft does; eight legs each with\0A\23 their own place in one ring is the same picture with no trigonometry in it.\0A\23\0A\23 A spider\27s body does not reach the ground and is not meant to\3A the legs are\0A\23 horizontal sticks and the gait swings them down. That is why the standing\0A\23 check in `Tests/entity_test.exe` asks whether a model FLOATS rather than\0A\23 whether it touches.\0A\0Aspider                  tex=64,32 scale=1.0 height=0.9 plate=1\0Aspider.thorax           at=-3,6,-3,3,12,3    pivot=0,9,0   uv=0,0               rgb=\232c2c38\0Aspider.head             at=-4,5,-11,4,13,-3  pivot=0,9,-3  uv=32,4  role=head   rgb=\2332323f\0Aspider.abdomen          at=-5,5,3,5,13,15    pivot=0,9,3   uv=0,12              rgb=\2326262f\0Aspider.rightLegOne      at=3,8,-5,19,10,-3   pivot=3,9,-4  uv=18,0  role=crawl turn=0 rgb=\2326262f\0Aspider.rightLegTwo      at=3,8,-2,19,10,0    pivot=3,9,-1  uv=18,0  role=crawl turn=1 rgb=\2326262f\0Aspider.rightLegThree    at=3,8,1,19,10,3     pivot=3,9,2   uv=18,0  role=crawl turn=2 rgb=\2326262f\0Aspider.rightLegFour     at=3,8,4,19,10,6     pivot=3,9,5   uv=18,0  role=crawl turn=3 rgb=\2326262f\0Aspider.leftLegOne       at=-19,8,-5,-3,10,-3 pivot=-3,9,-4 uv=18,0  role=crawl turn=4 rgb=\2326262f\0Aspider.leftLegTwo       at=-19,8,-2,-3,10,0  pivot=-3,9,-1 uv=18,0  role=crawl turn=5 rgb=\2326262f\0Aspider.leftLegThree     at=-19,8,1,-3,10,3   pivot=-3,9,2  uv=18,0  role=crawl turn=6 rgb=\2326262f\0Aspider.leftLegFour      at=-19,8,4,-3,10,6   pivot=-3,9,5  uv=18,0  role=crawl turn=7 rgb=\2326262f\0A\0A\23 --------------------------------------------------------------------------\0A\23 The animals.\0A\23\0A\23 **Read the `spin` note in `vxmodel.nim` before changing a body here.** A\0A\23 quadruped\27s barrel is authored STANDING UP and laid on its side by `spin=90`,\0A\23 because the picture is unwrapped from the box as authored\3A a cow\27s body is a\0A\23 box twelve wide, eighteen tall and ten deep, whose strip is forty-four pixels\0A\23 and fits, and the eighteen-deep box it looks like would want sixty and would\0A\23 run off the edge of a sixty-four pixel sheet. That is not a detail - it is\0A\23 why these rows and a real resource pack\27s picture can ever line up, and it is\0A\23 checked.\0A\23\0A\23 `scale=1.0` and not the player\27s 15/16\3A a quadruped model is already the\0A\23 height of the animal.\0A\0Apig                     tex=64,32 scale=1.0 height=0.9 plate=1\0Apig.trunk               at=-5,2,-4,5,18,4    pivot=0,10,0  uv=28,8  spin=90     rgb=\23d98c8c\0Apig.head                at=-4,6,-14,4,14,-6  pivot=0,12,-6 uv=0,0   role=head   rgb=\23dd9797\0Apig.snout               at=-2,8,-15,2,11,-14 pivot=0,12,-6 uv=16,16 of=head     rgb=\23c97c7c\0Apig.frontRightLeg       at=1,0,-7,5,6,-3     pivot=3,6,-5  uv=0,16  role=legR   turn=40 rgb=\23c97c7c\0Apig.frontLeftLeg        at=-5,0,-7,-1,6,-3   pivot=-3,6,-5 uv=0,16  role=legL   turn=40 rgb=\23c97c7c\0Apig.backRightLeg        at=1,0,3,5,6,7       pivot=3,6,5   uv=0,16  role=legL   turn=40 rgb=\23c97c7c\0Apig.backLeftLeg         at=-5,0,3,-1,6,7     pivot=-3,6,5  uv=0,16  role=legR   turn=40 rgb=\23c97c7c\0A\0Acow                     tex=64,32 scale=1.0 height=1.4 plate=1\0Acow.trunk               at=-6,8,-5,6,26,5    pivot=0,17,0  uv=18,4  spin=90     rgb=\234a3526\0Acow.head                at=-4,16,-15,4,24,-9 pivot=0,20,-9 uv=0,0   role=head   rgb=\234a3526\0Acow.rightHorn           at=4,23,-14,5,24,-13 pivot=0,20,-9 uv=22,0  of=head     rgb=\23d0d0d0\0Acow.leftHorn            at=-5,23,-14,-4,24,-13 pivot=0,20,-9 uv=22,0 of=head    rgb=\23d0d0d0\0Acow.frontRightLeg       at=2,0,-7,6,12,-3    pivot=4,12,-5 uv=0,16  role=legR   turn=40 rgb=\233d2b1f\0Acow.frontLeftLeg        at=-6,0,-7,-2,12,-3  pivot=-4,12,-5 uv=0,16 role=legL   turn=40 rgb=\233d2b1f\0Acow.backRightLeg        at=2,0,3,6,12,7      pivot=4,12,5  uv=0,16  role=legL   turn=40 rgb=\233d2b1f\0Acow.backLeftLeg         at=-6,0,3,-2,12,7    pivot=-4,12,5 uv=0,16  role=legR   turn=40 rgb=\233d2b1f\0A\0A\23 A sheep is a cow\27s layout on a smaller body. Its wool is the same boxes grown\0A\23 by a pixel - `grow` is what a hat layer is, and a sheep is the reason it is a\0A\23 field rather than a second set of boxes - and the wool boxes are not here\0A\23 because a second layer needs a second picture and nobody has granted one. See\0A\23 the note at the top of this file about what is and is not in this repository.\0Asheep                   tex=64,32 scale=1.0 height=1.3 plate=1\0Asheep.trunk             at=-4,7,-3,4,23,3    pivot=0,15,0  uv=28,8  spin=90     rgb=\23e8e8e8\0Asheep.head              at=-3,15,-16,3,21,-8 pivot=0,18,-8 uv=0,0   role=head   rgb=\23d6c4b0\0Asheep.frontRightLeg     at=1,0,-7,5,12,-3    pivot=3,12,-5 uv=0,16  role=legR   turn=40 rgb=\23d6c4b0\0Asheep.frontLeftLeg      at=-5,0,-7,-1,12,-3  pivot=-3,12,-5 uv=0,16 role=legL   turn=40 rgb=\23d6c4b0\0Asheep.backRightLeg      at=1,0,3,5,12,7      pivot=3,12,5  uv=0,16  role=legL   turn=40 rgb=\23d6c4b0\0Asheep.backLeftLeg       at=-5,0,3,-1,12,7    pivot=-3,12,5 uv=0,16  role=legR   turn=40 rgb=\23d6c4b0\0A\0A\23 A chicken\3A two thin legs, a beak and a wattle hung off the head, and two\0A\23 wings that flap rather than swing. The beak and the wattle hang off the head\0A\23 so that turning the head takes them with it - which is the whole reason a box\0A\23 may name a parent.\0Achicken                 tex=64,32 scale=1.0 height=0.8 plate=1\0Achicken.trunk           at=-3,4,-4,3,12,4    pivot=0,8,0   uv=0,9   spin=90     rgb=\23e8e8e8\0Achicken.head            at=-2,8,-5,2,14,-2   pivot=0,9,-2  uv=0,0   role=head   rgb=\23e8e8e8\0Achicken.beak            at=-2,10,-7,2,12,-5  pivot=0,9,-2  uv=14,0  of=head     rgb=\23e0a040\0Achicken.wattle          at=-1,8,-7,1,10,-5   pivot=0,9,-2  uv=14,4  of=head     rgb=\23c02020\0Achicken.rightLeg        at=0,0,-2,3,5,1      pivot=1,5,-1  uv=26,0  role=legR   turn=40 rgb=\23e0a040\0Achicken.leftLeg         at=-3,0,-2,0,5,1     pivot=-1,5,-1 uv=26,0  role=legL   turn=40 rgb=\23e0a040\0Achicken.rightWing       at=3,6,-3,4,10,3     pivot=3,10,0  uv=24,13 role=wingR  turn=60 rgb=\23d8d8d8\0Achicken.leftWing        at=-4,6,-3,-3,10,3   pivot=-3,10,0 uv=24,13 role=wingL  turn=60 rgb=\23d8d8d8\0A\0A\23 --------------------------------------------------------------------------\0A\23 The flat ones.\0A\23\0A\23 A dropped item and a thrown thing are not boxes - they are one square of a\0A\23 picture that turns to face you, which is what `flat` says and is what the\0A\23 chip particles already are. They carry no boxes at all and they are drawn in\0A\23 ONE batched mesh for all of them rather than as a part each\3A the same\0A\23 decision, for the same reason, as `vxfeel.meshFrameCost`.\0A\0Aitem                    flat=0.45,0.45 height=0.25\0Aarrow                   flat=0.5,0.16  height=0.16\0Asnowball                flat=0.3,0.3   height=0.25\0Afireball                flat=0.6,0.6   height=0.6\0A")))
